Subject: [PATCH] EE thread (FPS round 5): guest loads/stores with only main RAM inline, VU0 registers in SIMD
 locals, constant blends and repeated clamps folded, fewer pc stores, unchecked call targets

The game thread (EE) is the frame-rate limit in levels (TODO 94). Round 5 looked at the generated code itself.
Every change runs the same guest code in the same order with the same EE clock and gives the same bits: the
scripted cold boot into Amazon, the fight route (aim, walk, fire, melee, car) and Deep Amazon / Antarctica, all
in turbo with VIF1 on the EE thread, give the same game state on every game tick as before on the test PC,
the other PC and locally, and the native renderer's shots are pixel-identical.

ps2xRuntime (ps2_runtime_macros.h):
- Guest loads and stores (READn/WRITEn): only main RAM's usual case inline - an access wholly inside its 32 MB
  (and its uncached mirror at 0x20000000, PS2X_MEMORY_MIRROR_INLINE, default 1: the address with bit 29
  cleared) is read/written in place; everything else - the wrap-around at RAM's end, the other mirrors, the
  scratchpad, I/O, VU memory, the BIOS - goes to one out-of-line function per size (Ps2ReadSlowN /
  Ps2WriteSlowN) holding the former inline logic unchanged. The inline copies of those rare paths (two
  unrolled wrap-around byte loops, the special-address chain, the scratchpad test) made each access ~0.5-1 KB
  of code: the generated code was ~223 MB for 21,590 functions, now ~63 MB, and it compiles in ~5 min
  instead of ~23. PS2X_MEMORY_COLD_INLINE=1: as before. PS2X_MEMORY_SCRATCHPAD_OUTLINE=1: the scratchpad test
  (a non-constant scratchpad address) out of line as well (the port sets it). The out-of-line store tests the
  scratchpad first (it is special: what came next before). PS2X_HOT_STATS=1 also counts these out-of-line
  accesses (per 64 KB of address, listed with 64 added to the size).
- PS2_VADD_C/PS2_VSUB_C (an operand known clamped), FPU_ADD/SUB/MUL_S_KN/_NK/_KK (ps2_fpu_add_k etc.: a known
  clamped operand not clamped again; ps2_vclamp is idempotent - the same bits).
- ps2_register_cache.h: RC_VDECL/RC_VLD/RC_VST and RC_ADECL/RC_ALD/RC_AST - VU0's vf registers and ACC in
  SIMD locals.
- ps2_runtime.h: PS2_CALL_TARGET_IN (a function's table entry without the bounds test).

ps2xRecomp (options, each off unless the game's config turns it on):
- `vu0_register_cache` (register_cache.cpp): ctx->vu0_vf[n] and ctx->vu0_acc in SIMD locals rv_<n>/rvacc,
  written through to the context after the line that writes them, loaded again after a call (a VU0 micro
  program changes them) - as the FPU's registers. Before, every VU0 macro instruction loaded its operands from
  the context and stored its result there, and a guest store (which may alias the context for the C++
  compiler) forced the next instruction to load them again.
- `vu0_fold_blends` (foldVuConstantBlends): a VU0 instruction's destination-field blend with a constant mask
  (_mm_blendv_ps / PS2_VBLEND with _mm_set_epi32 of -1/0 - MSVC keeps it a variable blend) as _mm_blend_ps
  with an immediate, or no blend when every field is written; PS2_VADD/PS2_VSUB(x, mul_res) after
  `mul_res = PS2_VMUL...` as PS2_VADD_C/PS2_VSUB_C.
- `fpu_clamp_elision` (elideFpuClamps, after the register cache): an FPU ADD/SUB/MUL operand that is clamped
  already - an rf_<n> last written by ADD/SUB/MUL/DIV/SQRT/RSQRT or a MOV/NEG/ABS/MAX/MIN of such, the ACC (only
  FPU_SET_ACC writes it, always with such a result), a nested ADD/SUB/MUL - not clamped again. Followed line
  by line: a label and a reload after a call forget, a block's end keeps what both ways agree on.
- `minimal_goto_pc_stores` (with minimal_pc_stores): ctx->pc not stored before a forward jump inside the
  function - nothing at its label reads it before storing it, except a branch whose delay slot is also entered
  (its `if (ctx->pc == <delay slot>)`), whose labels keep the store (ControlFlowAnalysisResult::pcReadLabels).
  Backward jumps keep it for their loop checkpoint.
- `direct_calls_unchecked` (with direct_calls): a jal's table entry read with PS2_CALL_TARGET_IN - a known
  target is a function of the table, which spans every function (checked for this game: 95,638 sites, all
  inside the table and on a filled slot).

Measured (test PC GTX 1080 / the other PC RX 5700 XT, Amazon from its first gate, the user's settings at 1080
lines, 240 held, game ticks 8600-13000): see TODO 94's round-5 paragraph.
